# MinimalTodo — Full Project Documentation
### *"Everything you need to learn PHP, HTML, JS and this project — from scratch"*

---

> **How to use this doc**: Read it section by section. Every concept is explained before it is used in the project. By the end, you should be able to re-write the project yourself and answer any viva question confidently.

---

# PART 1: What is PHP?

## 1.1 The Big Picture — How Websites Work

When you type `http://localhost:8000` in your browser, this happens:
1. Your browser sends an **HTTP Request** to the server: *"Give me index.php"*
2. The server (PHP built-in server in this case) picks up the file
3. PHP **executes** the code in the file on the server
4. PHP sends back **plain HTML text** to the browser
5. Your browser reads that HTML and displays it

> **Key Insight**: PHP runs on the **server** (your computer acting as a server). JavaScript runs in the **browser**. They are in different places and communicate over HTTP.

## 1.2 What is PHP?

PHP stands for **Hypertext Preprocessor** (recursive acronym). It is a **server-side scripting language**, meaning:
- The user never sees your PHP code
- PHP processes data, files, databases etc. on the server
- It outputs HTML/JSON/text back to the browser

PHP files use the [.php](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php) extension. A PHP file can contain a mix of PHP code and HTML.

## 1.3 PHP Syntax Rules — The Complete Primer

### Rule 1: PHP Tags
All PHP code must be wrapped in open/close tags:
```php
<?php
  // PHP code goes here
?>
```
Everything outside these tags is treated as plain HTML and sent directly to the browser.

### Rule 2: Statements end with `;`
Every PHP statement (instruction) ends with a semicolon, just like a period at the end of a sentence.
```php
$name = "Harsh";   // This is one statement
echo $name;        // This is another statement
```

### Rule 3: Variables start with `$`
```php
$age = 21;
$name = "Harsh";
$isStudent = true;
```

### Rule 4: Data Types
```php
$integer   = 42;           // Whole number
$float     = 3.14;         // Decimal number
$string    = "Hello";      // Text (in quotes)
$boolean   = true;         // true or false
$array     = [1, 2, 3];    // A list of values
$null      = null;         // No value / empty
```

### Rule 5: String Concatenation
Use the `.` (dot) operator to join strings:
```php
$first = "Hello";
$second = "World";
echo $first . " " . $second;  // Outputs: Hello World
```

### Rule 6: Conditional Logic
```php
if ($age >= 18) {
    echo "Adult";
} elseif ($age >= 13) {
    echo "Teenager";
} else {
    echo "Child";
}
```

### Rule 7: Functions
A function is a reusable block of code:
```php
function greet($personName) {
    return "Hello, " . $personName . "!";
}
echo greet("Harsh");  // Outputs: Hello, Harsh!
```

### Rule 8: `global` keyword
If a variable is defined outside a function, the function cannot see it by default. You must use `global` to bring it in:
```php
$myGlobal = "I am global";

function testFunc() {
    global $myGlobal;       // Now I can access it
    echo $myGlobal;         // Works!
}
```

### Rule 9: Arrays (Lists)
```php
// Indexed Array (like a numbered list)
$fruits = ["apple", "banana", "mango"];
echo $fruits[0];  // apple (index starts at 0)

// Associative Array (like a dictionary)
$task = [
    "id"     => "abc123",
    "title"  => "Buy milk",
    "status" => "todo"
];
echo $task["title"];  // Buy milk
```

### Rule 10: Built-in Functions used in this project

| Function | What it does |
|---|---|
| `file_exists($path)` | Returns `true` if the file exists on the disk |
| `file_get_contents($path)` | Reads an entire file and returns its content as a string |
| `file_put_contents($path, $data)` | Writes a string to a file (creates it if missing, overwrites if exists) |
| `json_decode($string, true)` | Converts a JSON string into a PHP associative array |
| `json_encode($array)` | Converts a PHP array into a JSON string |
| `isset($var)` | Returns `true` if a variable exists and is not `null` |
| `header($string)` | Sends an HTTP header to the browser before any output |

