# PART 3: [index.php](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php) — Line by Line

## 3.1 The PHP Block (Lines 1–39)

This entire block runs on the server before anything is shown to the user.

### Line 1: Opening PHP Tag
```php
<?php
```
Everything after this is PHP code until the closing `?>` tag.

---

### Line 2: Setting the Content-Type Header
```php
header('Content-Type: text/html; charset=UTF-8');
```
**What is a Header?** An HTTP response has two parts: **Headers** (meta-information) and **Body** (actual content). Here we tell the browser:
- `Content-Type: text/html` → "I am sending you HTML"
- `charset=UTF-8` → "The text is encoded in UTF-8" (supports all languages/special characters)

> **Rule**: `header()` must be called BEFORE any output (`echo`, HTML etc.), otherwise PHP throws an error.

---

### Line 4: Defining the Tasks File Path
```php
$tasksFile = 'tasks.json';
```
We store the filename in a variable so if we ever want to rename the file, we change it in one place. This is a good programming practice called **DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)**.

---

### Lines 6–12: The [loadTasks()](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#6-13) Function

```php
function loadTasks() {
    global $tasksFile;
    if (!file_exists($tasksFile)) {
        file_put_contents($tasksFile, '[]');
    }
    return json_decode(file_get_contents($tasksFile), true) ?: [];
}
```

Let's break this down **piece by piece**:

#### `global $tasksFile;`
The variable `$tasksFile` was defined outside the function (at line 4). PHP functions have their own **scope** — they can't see variables from outside. The `global` keyword imports it.

#### `if (!file_exists($tasksFile))`
- `file_exists($tasksFile)` → checks if [tasks.json](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/tasks.json) exists. Returns `true` or `false`.
- The `!` (NOT operator) flips it: `!true` = `false`, `!false` = `true`
- So: "**if** the file does **NOT** exist, run the block"

#### `file_put_contents($tasksFile, '[]');`
- Creates [tasks.json](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/tasks.json) and writes `[]` into it
- `[]` is an **empty JSON array** — an empty list of tasks
- This is the "first run" initialization

#### `return json_decode(file_get_contents($tasksFile), true) ?: [];`
This is a chain of operations. Let's read it inside-out:

1. `file_get_contents($tasksFile)` → Reads [tasks.json](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/tasks.json). Returns a string like `[{"id":"abc","title":"Task 1"}]`
2. `json_decode(..., true)` → Converts the JSON string to a PHP array. The `true` means "give me an associative array (dictionary), not an object"
3. `?: []` → This is the **Null Coalescing Elvis Operator**. If `json_decode` returns `null` (file was empty/corrupted), use `[]` (empty array) instead as a fallback.
4. `return` → Sends this value back to whoever called the function.

---

### Lines 14–17: The [saveTasks()](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#14-18) Function

```php
function saveTasks($tasks) {
    global $tasksFile;
    file_put_contents($tasksFile, json_encode($tasks, JSON_PRETTY_PRINT));
}
```

#### `$tasks` parameter
This function accepts a PHP array of tasks as input (called a **parameter**).

#### `json_encode($tasks, JSON_PRETTY_PRINT)`
1. `json_encode($tasks)` → Converts the PHP array back to a JSON string
2. `JSON_PRETTY_PRINT` → A **flag** (option) that formats the JSON with indentation so it's human-readable in the file:
```json
[
    {
        "id": "abc123",
        "title": "Buy milk"
    }
]
```
Without this flag it would all be one line: `[{"id":"abc123","title":"Buy milk"}]`

#### `file_put_contents($tasksFile, ...)`
Writes the JSON string to [tasks.json](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/tasks.json). **Overwrites** the entire file each time — this is intentional (the whole array is always saved fresh).

---

### Line 19: Getting the HTTP Method
```php
$method = $_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'];
```

#### `$_SERVER`
This is a PHP **Superglobal** — a special pre-built variable that contains information about the server and the current HTTP request. It is always available everywhere in PHP.

**Common `$_SERVER` values:**

| Key | What it contains |
|---|---|
| `$_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD']` | `'GET'` or `'POST'` |
| `$_SERVER['REQUEST_URI']` | The URL path (e.g., `/index.php?api=tasks`) |
| `$_SERVER['HTTP_HOST']` | The domain (e.g., `localhost:8000`) |

In this project, when JavaScript calls `fetch('?api=tasks')`, the method is `GET`. When it calls `fetch('', {method: 'POST', ...})`, the method is `POST`.

---

### Lines 21–26: The GET API Handler

```php
if ($method === 'GET' && isset($_GET['api'])) {
    if ($_GET['api'] === 'tasks') {
        echo json_encode(loadTasks());
    }
    exit;
}
```

#### `$_GET`
Another PHP Superglobal. It captures **URL query parameters**. For the URL `index.php?api=tasks`:
- `$_GET['api']` = `"tasks"`

#### `===` vs `==`
- `==` checks if values are equal (loose comparison): `"1" == 1` is `true`
- `===` checks if values AND types are equal (strict): `"1" === 1` is `false`
Always use `===` to avoid unexpected bugs.

#### `&&` (AND operator)
Both conditions must be true:
- `$method === 'GET'` → Is it a GET request?
- `isset($_GET['api'])` → Does the URL have an `?api=something` parameter?

#### `echo json_encode(loadTasks());`
If both conditions are true and the api value is `'tasks'`:
1. Call [loadTasks()](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#6-13) → returns a PHP array
2. `json_encode(...)` → converts that PHP array to a JSON string
3. `echo` → prints that JSON string to the HTTP response body
4. JavaScript receives this JSON string as the response to its `fetch()` call

#### `exit;`
**Crucially important!** This stops PHP from executing any further code. Without it, PHP would continue and print the entire HTML page *after* the JSON, which would corrupt the response.

---

### Lines 28–36: The POST API Handler

```php
if ($method === 'POST') {
    $input = json_decode(file_get_contents('php://input'), true);
    
    if (isset($input['action']) && $input['action'] === 'save') {
        saveTasks($input['tasks']);
        echo json_encode(['success' => true]);
        exit;
    }
}
```

#### `file_get_contents('php://input')`
When JavaScript sends a POST request with JSON body:
```javascript
fetch('', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
    body: JSON.stringify({action: 'save', tasks})
});
```
The raw JSON body arrives at the server. `php://input` is a **special PHP stream** that gives access to that raw body data. It's like a "pipe" that reads the incoming request body.

#### `$input = json_decode(..., true)`
Converts the raw JSON body string into a PHP associative array:
```php
// $input now looks like:
[
    'action' => 'save',
    'tasks' => [
        ['id' => 'abc', 'title' => 'Task 1', ...],
        ...
    ]
]
```

#### [saveTasks($input['tasks'])](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#14-18)
Calls our [saveTasks](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#14-18) function with the array of tasks received from the browser.

#### `echo json_encode(['success' => true])`
Sends back a JSON response: `{"success": true}`. JavaScript can check this to know the save was successful.

---

### Line 38: Loading tasks for initial render
```php
$tasks = loadTasks();
```
This loads tasks into `$tasks` for any initial server-side rendering (though in this project, tasks are loaded again by JavaScript after page load, so this line is mostly for potential server-side use).

---

### Line 39: Closing PHP Tag
```php
?>
```
After this, everything is plain HTML that gets sent directly to the browser.

---

## 3.2 The HTML Structure (Lines 40–139)

HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the skeleton of the web page. Every visual element is an HTML **tag** (element).

### Basic HTML Anatomy
```html
<tagname attribute="value">Content goes here</tagname>
```
- **Opening tag**: `<tagname>`
- **Closing tag**: `</tagname>`
- **Self-closing tag** (no content): `<input type="text">`
- **Attributes**: Extra information about the element

### Lines 40–47: The Document Setup
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>MinimalTodo</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/styles.css">
</head>
```

| Element | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `<!DOCTYPE html>` | Tells the browser this is an HTML5 document (not HTML4 or XHTML) |
| `<html lang="en">` | Root element. `lang="en"` tells screen readers the language |
| `<head>` | Contains metadata (not visible to user) |
| `<meta charset="UTF-8">` | Sets character encoding (same as the PHP header we sent) |
| `<meta name="viewport" ...>` | Makes the site responsive on mobile screens |
| `<title>MinimalTodo</title>` | The text shown in the browser tab |
| `<link rel="stylesheet" href="css/styles.css">` | Loads our CSS file |

### Lines 48–55: The Header
```html
<header>
    <h1>MinimalTodo</h1>
    <div class="view-toggle">
        <button class="view-btn active" data-view="kanban">Kanban</button>
        <button class="view-btn" data-view="calendar">Calendar</button>
    </div>
</header>
```

#### `<header>`
A semantic HTML5 element. "Semantic" means the tag name carries meaning — `<header>` tells developers (and search engines) this is the top section of the page, not just a generic `<div>`.

#### `<h1>`
Heading Level 1 — the most important heading on the page. SEO best practice: one `<h1>` per page.

#### `data-view="kanban"` — Custom Data Attributes
The `data-*` pattern is a standard HTML feature. You can attach any custom data to an element using `data-yourname="value"`. JavaScript reads these using:
```javascript
button.dataset.view  // returns "kanban"
```

#### `class="view-btn active"`
Multiple CSS classes can be applied to one element, separated by spaces. CSS then styles it using `.view-btn` and `.active` rules.

---

### Lines 57–98: Main Content Area

#### The Kanban View (Lines 58–85)
```html
<div class="kanban-view active" id="kanbanView">
    <div class="column" data-status="todo">
        <div class="column-header">
            <span>Todo</span>
            <span class="column-count" id="todoCount">0</span>
        </div>
        <div class="task-list" id="todoList"></div>
        <button class="add-task-btn" data-status="todo">+ Add Task</button>
    </div>
    <!-- ... inprogress column ... -->
    <!-- ... done column ... -->
</div>
```

- `id="kanbanView"` → A unique identifier on the page. JavaScript uses `document.getElementById('kanbanView')` to grab it.
- `<div class="task-list" id="todoList"></div>` → This div starts **empty**! JavaScript will inject task cards into it.
- `data-status="todo"` → Used by drag-and-drop logic to know which column a task was dropped into.

#### The Calendar View (Lines 87–97)
```html
<div class="calendar-view" id="calendarView">
    <div class="calendar-header">
        <div class="calendar-nav">
            <button id="prevMonth">&lt;</button>
            <span class="calendar-month" id="calendarMonth"></span>
            <button id="nextMonth">&gt;</button>
        </div>
        <button class="btn btn-secondary" id="todayBtn">Today</button>
    </div>
    <div class="calendar-grid" id="calendarGrid"></div>
</div>
```

- `&lt;` and `&gt;` are **HTML Entities** — special codes for `<` and `>`. Because `<` and `>` are used for HTML tags, we need to escape them: `&lt;` = Less Than, `&gt;` = Greater Than.
- `id="calendarGrid"` → Also starts empty. JavaScript builds the entire calendar grid dynamically.

