## 3.3 The JavaScript Block (Lines 141–517)

This is the "brain" of the UI. It runs entirely in the **browser**, not on the server.

### 3.3.1 State Variables (Lines 142–145)

```javascript
let tasks = [];
let currentView = 'kanban';
let editingTaskId = null;
let calendarDate = new Date();
```

These are the application's **state** — the current data held in memory:

| Variable | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `tasks` | Array of all task objects (loaded from PHP API) |
| `currentView` | Which view is active: `'kanban'` or `'calendar'` |
| `editingTaskId` | The `id` of the task being edited in the modal (`null` if adding new) |
| `calendarDate` | A `Date` object representing which month the calendar is showing |

#### `let` vs `const` vs `var`
- `let` → Can be reassigned. Used for values that change.
- `const` → Cannot be reassigned. Used for values that are fixed.
- `var` → Old way. Has quirky "hoisting" behavior. Avoid in modern JS.

---

### 3.3.2 DOM References (Lines 147–148)

```javascript
const modal = document.getElementById('taskModal');
const subtasksList = document.getElementById('subtasksList');
```

`document.getElementById('id')` → Finds an HTML element by its `id` attribute and returns a reference to it. We store these in variables so we don't have to search the DOM every time we need them.

**The DOM (Document Object Model)** is a tree-like structure that the browser builds from the HTML. JavaScript can read and modify it.

---

### 3.3.3 `generateId()` — Creating Unique IDs (Lines 150–152)

```javascript
function generateId() {
    return Date.now().toString(36) + Math.random().toString(36).substr(2);
}
```

Every task and subtask needs a unique identifier. Instead of a database auto-increment, we generate one client-side:

- `Date.now()` → Current timestamp in milliseconds (e.g., `1709548000000`)
- `.toString(36)` → Converts to Base-36 (numbers + a-z letters). Makes it shorter: `"mm9k04v9"` instead of `"1709548000000"`
- `Math.random().toString(36).substr(2)` → Random number also converted to Base-36, `.substr(2)` removes the leading `"0."` 
- Concatenating both gives a unique ID like `"mm9k04v9s0ecjqnj4d"`

---

### 3.3.4 [loadTasks()](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#6-13) — Fetching Data from PHP (Lines 154–158)

```javascript
async function loadTasks() {
    const res = await fetch('?api=tasks');
    tasks = await res.json();
    render();
}
```

#### `async` / `await`
These keywords handle **asynchronous operations** — things that take time (like a network request). Without them, JavaScript would not wait for the response and would try to use `tasks` before it's loaded.

- `async function` → Marks a function as asynchronous
- `await` → Pauses this function until the Promise resolves (the response arrives)

#### `fetch('?api=tasks')`
The `fetch()` API makes an HTTP request from JavaScript, just like your browser makes requests for pages.
- URL `'?api=tasks'` → Relative URL, resolves to `http://localhost:8000/index.php?api=tasks`
- This triggers the GET handler in PHP (lines 21–26 of index.php)
- Returns a **Promise** that resolves when the response arrives

#### `res.json()`
The `res` (response) object has a `.json()` method that reads the response body and parses it as JSON. It also returns a Promise, so we `await` it too.

**Data flow**: PHP `echo json_encode(loadTasks())` → HTTP response body (`[{"id":"...", "title":"..."}]`) → `fetch()` receives it → `res.json()` parses it → `tasks` array is populated.

---

### 3.3.5 [saveTasks()](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#14-18) — Sending Data to PHP (Lines 160–166)

```javascript
async function saveTasks() {
    await fetch('', {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
        body: JSON.stringify({action: 'save', tasks})
    });
}
```

#### `fetch('', { ... })` with POST
- URL `''` → Empty string means the same URL as the current page ([index.php](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php))
- `method: 'POST'` → Tells PHP this is a POST request
- `headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'}` → Tells the server we're sending JSON
- `body: JSON.stringify({action: 'save', tasks})` → Converts our JS object to a JSON string for the request body

#### `JSON.stringify({action: 'save', tasks})`
`{ action: 'save', tasks }` → ES6 shorthand for `{ action: 'save', tasks: tasks }`
Result (example): `'{"action":"save","tasks":[{"id":"abc","title":"Task 1",...}]}'`

PHP receives this string, `json_decode`s it, and calls [saveTasks()](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#14-18).

---

### 3.3.6 `render()` — Top-Level Render (Lines 168–171)

```javascript
function render() {
    renderKanban();
    renderCalendar();
}
```

This is the **main render function**. Whenever the `tasks` array changes, call this to update both views.

---

### 3.3.7 `renderKanban()` — Building the Board (Lines 173–197)

```javascript
function renderKanban() {
    const columns = {
        todo:       document.getElementById('todoList'),
        inprogress: document.getElementById('inprogressList'),
        done:       document.getElementById('doneList')
    };
    
    Object.keys(columns).forEach(status => {
        columns[status].innerHTML = '';  // Clear existing cards
    });
    
    const counts = {todo: 0, inprogress: 0, done: 0};
    
    tasks.forEach(task => {
        const card = createTaskCard(task);
        if (columns[task.status]) {
            columns[task.status].appendChild(card);
            counts[task.status]++;
        }
    });
    
    document.getElementById('todoCount').textContent = counts.todo;
    document.getElementById('inprogressCount').textContent = counts.inprogress;
    document.getElementById('doneCount').textContent = counts.done;
}
```

#### `Object.keys(columns).forEach(...)`
- `Object.keys(obj)` → Returns an array of the object's keys: `['todo', 'inprogress', 'done']`
- `.forEach(status => { ... })` → Loops through each key (status name). Arrow functions `=>` are a concise way to write functions.

#### `element.innerHTML = ''`
Clears all child HTML inside the element — before re-rendering to avoid duplicates.

#### `tasks.forEach(task => { ... })`
Loops through every task object in the array. For each task, creates a card DOM element and appends it to the correct column based on `task.status`.

#### `columns[task.status].appendChild(card)`
This is dynamic property access. If `task.status` is `'todo'`, then `columns['todo']` is the todo column element.

#### `.textContent` vs `.innerHTML`
- `.textContent` → Sets/gets plain text. Safer — does not parse HTML.
- `.innerHTML` → Sets/gets HTML. Allows embedding tags but can be a security risk (XSS) if user input is placed here directly.

---

### 3.3.8 `createTaskCard()` — Building Each Card (Lines 199–260)

```javascript
function createTaskCard(task) {
    const card = document.createElement('div');
    card.className = 'task-card';
    card.draggable = true;
    card.dataset.id = task.id;
    
    // --- Due date color logic ---
    let dueDateClass = '';
    if (task.dueDate) {
        const due = new Date(task.dueDate);
        const today = new Date();
        today.setHours(0, 0, 0, 0);
        if (due < today) dueDateClass = 'overdue';
        else if ((due - today) <= 3 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000) dueDateClass = 'soon';
    }
    // ...
}
```

#### `document.createElement('div')`
Creates a new `<div>` element in memory (not yet on the page).

#### `card.className = 'task-card'`
Sets the CSS class. Equivalent to `class="task-card"` in HTML.

#### `card.draggable = true`
Makes the element draggable by the browser's built-in drag-and-drop system.

#### `card.dataset.id = task.id`
Sets a `data-id` attribute on the element. Used later to identify which task is being dragged.

#### Due Date Color Logic
```javascript
const due = new Date(task.dueDate);  // e.g. "2026-03-10" → Date object
const today = new Date();            // Current date and time
today.setHours(0, 0, 0, 0);         // Reset time to midnight (for fair comparison)

if (due < today) dueDateClass = 'overdue';                             // Past due
else if ((due - today) <= 3 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000) dueDateClass = 'soon'; // Within 3 days
```

`due - today` → In JavaScript, subtracting two `Date` objects gives the difference in **milliseconds**. We compare it to:
- `3 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000` = 3 days × 24 hours × 60 min × 60 sec × 1000 ms = **259,200,000 ms**

#### Building Subtask HTML
```javascript
let subtasksHtml = '';
if (task.subtasks && task.subtasks.length > 0) {
    subtasksHtml = '<div class="task-card-subtasks">';
    task.subtasks.forEach(subtask => {
        subtasksHtml += `
            <div class="task-card-subtask ${subtask.done ? 'done' : ''}">
                <input type="checkbox" ${subtask.done ? 'checked' : ''} data-subtask-id="${subtask.id}">
                <span>${escapeHtml(subtask.title)}</span>
            </div>
        `;
    });
    subtasksHtml += '</div>';
}
```

#### Template Literals (Backtick Strings)
`` `Hello ${name}` `` → Backtick strings allow **string interpolation**: embedding variables/expressions inside `${}`. Much cleaner than concatenation.

#### Ternary Operator
`condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse`
- `subtask.done ? 'done' : ''` → If done, add `'done'` class; else add empty string

#### Setting the card's HTML
```javascript
card.innerHTML = `
    <div class="task-card-title">${escapeHtml(task.title)}</div>
    ${subtasksHtml}
    <div class="task-card-meta">
        ${task.dueDate ? `<span class="task-card-due ${dueDateClass}">📅 ${formatDate(task.dueDate)}</span>` : ''}
    </div>
`;
```

