### 3.3.9 Event Listeners on Cards (Lines 236–257)

```javascript
card.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    if (e.target.type === 'checkbox') {
        e.stopPropagation();
        const subtaskId = e.target.dataset.subtaskId;
        const subtask = task.subtasks.find(s => s.id === subtaskId);
        if (subtask) {
            subtask.done = e.target.checked;
            saveTasks().then(render);
        }
    } else {
        openTaskModal(task);
    }
});
```

#### `addEventListener(event, callback)`
- `event` → A string naming the event: `'click'`, `'dragstart'`, `'drop'`, etc.
- `callback` → A function to execute when the event fires. [(e)](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#6-13) is the event object.

#### `e.target`
`e.target` is the actual element that was clicked (could be a child element). `e.currentTarget` would be the element the listener is on (`card` here).

#### `e.stopPropagation()`
Events **bubble up** the DOM — a click on a checkbox would also trigger a click on the parent card. `stopPropagation()` prevents this, so clicking the checkbox doesn't also open the modal.

#### `task.subtasks.find(s => s.id === subtaskId)`
`.find()` loops through an array and returns the **first element** that satisfies the condition. If nothing matches, it returns `undefined`.

#### [saveTasks().then(render)](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#14-18)
[saveTasks()](file:///c:/Users/Harsh%20Y%20Dhakan/Desktop/php%20stuff/index.php#14-18) returns a Promise. `.then(render)` means: "After saving is complete, call `render()`". This ensures the board updates only after data is persisted.

---

### 3.3.10 Drag and Drop (Lines 250–278)

#### On the Card
```javascript
card.addEventListener('dragstart', (e) => {
    card.classList.add('dragging');        // Visual: make card semi-transparent
    e.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', task.id);  // Store task ID
});

card.addEventListener('dragend', () => {
    card.classList.remove('dragging');     // Remove visual effect
});
```

`e.dataTransfer` is a special object for drag-and-drop operations — a "clipboard" that holds data during the drag.

#### On the Columns
```javascript
document.querySelectorAll('.column').forEach(column => {
    column.addEventListener('dragover', (e) => {
        e.preventDefault();   // REQUIRED: allows dropping
    });
    
    column.addEventListener('drop', (e) => {
        e.preventDefault();
        const taskId = e.dataTransfer.getData('text/plain');  // Retrieve task ID
        const newStatus = column.dataset.status;             // Get column's status
        
        const task = tasks.find(t => t.id === taskId);
        if (task && task.status !== newStatus) {
            task.status = newStatus;       // Mutate the task's status
            saveTasks().then(render);      // Save and re-render
        }
    });
});
```

- `dragover` + `e.preventDefault()` → By default, browsers don't allow dropping. Calling `preventDefault()` on `dragover` enables drops.
- `getData()` → Retrieves the task ID stored during `dragstart`.
- `column.dataset.status` → Gets the `data-status` attribute value (`'todo'`, `'inprogress'`, or `'done'`).

---

### 3.3.11 `renderCalendar()` — Building the Calendar (Lines 280–344)

```javascript
function renderCalendar() {
    const grid = document.getElementById('calendarGrid');
    const monthLabel = document.getElementById('calendarMonth');
    
    const year = calendarDate.getFullYear();   // e.g. 2026
    const month = calendarDate.getMonth();     // 0-indexed! Jan=0, Dec=11
    
    monthLabel.textContent = new Date(year, month).toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
        month: 'long', year: 'numeric'
    });   // e.g. "March 2026"
    
    const firstDay = new Date(year, month, 1);       // First day of current month
    const startDate = new Date(firstDay);
    startDate.setDate(startDate.getDate() - firstDay.getDay());  // Go back to Sunday
    
    // ... build grid
}
```

#### JavaScript Date Object

| Method | Returns |
|---|---|
| `new Date()` | Current date & time |
| `new Date('2026-03-10')` | Specific date |
| `.getFullYear()` | Year (e.g., 2026) |
| `.getMonth()` | Month 0–11 (January = 0!) |
| `.getDate()` | Day of month (1–31) |
| `.getDay()` | Day of week 0–6 (Sunday = 0) |
| `.setDate(n)` | Sets the day of month |
| `.toISOString()` | `"2026-03-10T00:00:00.000Z"` |

#### Calendar Start Calculation
```javascript
const firstDay = new Date(year, month, 1);      // e.g., March 1, 2026 (Saturday, day 6)
startDate.setDate(startDate.getDate() - firstDay.getDay());
// getDay() of March 1 = 6 (Saturday)
// setDate(1 - 6) = setDate(-5) → Feb 23, 2026 (the preceding Sunday)
```
This finds the **Sunday of the week containing the 1st of the month** — where the calendar grid starts.

#### Creating Day Cells
```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 42; i++) {     // 42 = 6 rows × 7 columns
    const date = new Date(startDate);
    date.setDate(startDate.getDate() + i);   // Increment day by i
    
    const cell = document.createElement('div');
    cell.className = 'calendar-day';
    
    if (date.getMonth() !== month) {
        cell.classList.add('other-month');   // Greyed out days from prev/next month
    }
    if (date.getTime() === today.getTime()) {
        cell.classList.add('today');         // Highlighted today
    }
    
    const dateStr = date.toISOString().split('T')[0]; // "2026-03-10"
    const dayTasks = tasks.filter(t => t.dueDate === dateStr);  // Tasks due on this date
    
    dayTasks.forEach(task => {
        const taskEl = document.createElement('div');
        taskEl.className = 'calendar-task' + (task.status === 'done' ? ' done' : '');
        taskEl.textContent = task.title;
        cell.appendChild(taskEl);
    });
    
    grid.appendChild(cell);
}
```

#### `.filter()`
Returns a **new array** containing only elements that match the condition:
```javascript
tasks.filter(t => t.dueDate === dateStr)
// Returns: all tasks where dueDate equals this cell's date
```

#### `toISOString().split('T')[0]`
- `toISOString()` returns `"2026-03-10T00:00:00.000Z"`
- `.split('T')` splits on `'T'` → `["2026-03-10", "00:00:00.000Z"]`
- `[0]` takes the first part → `"2026-03-10"`

