# PART 6: Complete Data Flow Diagrams

## 6.1 App Load
```
Browser opens localhost:8000
         ↓
PHP runs, outputs HTML page
         ↓
Browser renders HTML (empty columns)
         ↓
JavaScript runs: loadTasks()
         ↓
fetch('?api=tasks') → HTTP GET → PHP
         ↓
PHP: echo json_encode(loadTasks())
         ↓
Response: '[{"id":"...","title":"..."}]'
         ↓
JavaScript: tasks = await res.json()
         ↓
render() → renderKanban() + renderCalendar()
         ↓
Task cards appear in columns
```

## 6.2 Adding a Task
```
User clicks "+ Add Task" button
         ↓
openTaskModal(null, null, 'todo') called
         ↓
Modal appears with empty fields
         ↓
User fills in title, due date, subtasks
         ↓
User clicks "Save"
         ↓
Validation: title empty? → alert & stop
         ↓
tasks.push({ new task object })
         ↓
saveTasks() → fetch POST → PHP
         ↓
PHP: json_decode body → saveTasks($tasks)
         ↓
PHP: file_put_contents('tasks.json', newJson)
         ↓
Response: {"success": true}
         ↓
modal.classList.remove('active') → modal hides
render() → board updates with new card
```

## 6.3 Drag and Drop
```
User grabs card → dragstart event
         ↓
card gets 'dragging' class (opacity 0.5)
e.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', taskId)
         ↓
User hovers over column → dragover event
e.preventDefault() [enables drop]
         ↓
User releases → drop event
e.dataTransfer.getData('text/plain') → taskId
column.dataset.status → newStatus
         ↓
task = tasks.find(t => t.id === taskId)
task.status = newStatus
         ↓
saveTasks().then(render)
```

